        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.046,
            0.364,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.088,
            0.401,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": "How do we solve this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.118,
            0.564,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.199,
            0.211,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.418,
            0.228,
            0.578,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{1}{5} \\div \\frac{1}{2} = 2 \\times \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.263,
            0.33,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "What is $\\frac { 2 } { 3 } \\div \\frac { 3 } { 5 } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.3,
            0.585,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "Rewriting this as multiplication, we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.331,
            0.544,
            0.367
          ],
          "content": "{ \\frac { 3 } { 5 } } \\times ? = { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.362,
            0.414,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "How will we solve this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.434,
            0.398,
            0.569,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.492,
            0.211,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.412,
            0.521,
            0.583,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{2}{3} \\div \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{5}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{10}{9}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.573,
            0.288,
            0.593
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Discussion"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.605,
            0.846,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": "In each of the division problems above, observe how we found the answer. Can we frame a rule that tells us how to divide two fractions?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.643,
            0.534,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider the previous problem."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.661,
            0.546,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": "In every division problem we have a dividend, divisor and quotient. The technique we have been using to get the quotient is:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.739,
            0.545,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": "1. \tFirst, find the number which gives 1 when multiplied by the divisor. We see that the resulting number is a fraction whose numerator is the"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.576,
            0.651,
            0.846,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.813,
            0.847,
            0.915
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "divisor’s denominator and denominator is the divisor’s numerator. For the divisor $\\frac{3}{5}$ this fraction is ${ \\frac { 5 } { 3 } } .$ We call $\\frac { 5 } { 3 }$ the reciprocal of $\\frac{3}{5},$ When we multiply a fraction by its reciprocal, we get 1. So, the first step in our technique is to find the divisor’s reciprocal."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.938,
            0.192,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": "188"
        },
